  "new_commit_message": "fix(supabase): recommend scoped personal access tokens for the Management API, CLI, and MCP (#604)\n\nThe skill had no guidance on personal access tokens, so agents setting up\nManagement API scripts, CLI in CI, or the MCP server without OAuth tended to\nsuggest a classic token with full account access. Scoped personal access\ntokens limit a token to chosen organizations, projects, and permissions.\n\nChanges to skills/supabase/SKILL.md:\n\n- Security checklist: prefer scoped personal access tokens for scripts, CI,\n  and agents, and look up required permissions in the personal access tokens\n  guide instead of guessing. No endpoint-to-permission mapping is copied into\n  the skill, so it can't drift from the docs.\n- CLI gotchas: the browser flow of `supabase login` creates a classic token.\n  In CI, set SUPABASE_ACCESS_TOKEN to a scoped token, which takes precedence\n  over a saved login. Commands that connect with the database password (for\n  example `supabase db push` with SUPABASE_DB_PASSWORD) aren't limited by the\n  token's permissions.\n- MCP troubleshooting: where the browser OAuth flow can't run, pass a scoped\n  token in the Authorization header. New step 4 explains the \"You do not have\n  permission to perform this action\" error a scoped token returns when it\n  lacks a tool's permission or the project is outside its scope.",
